<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1645">1645</a>–<a href="/years/1700">1700</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora was a Mexican scholar, poet and priest, one of the leading intellectuals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. A professor of mathematics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-and-pontifical-university-of-mexico">Royal University of Mexico</a>, he wrote on astronomy, history and geography, rejected astrological views of comets, collected Aztec <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and drew a map of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>. He was a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sor-juana-ines-de-la-cruz">Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlos-de-siguenza-y-gongora.jpg" alt="Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D. Alfredo Chavero · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Don_Carlos_de_Sig%C3%BCenza_y_G%C3%B3ngora.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sigüenza was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 14 August 1645, the eldest son among nine children of a father from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, who had tutored the Spanish royal family, and a mother born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, through whom he was related to the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-gongora">Luis de Góngora</a>. He entered the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as a novice in 1660 but was dismissed in 1668 for repeatedly breaking its discipline by going out at night, and his plea to return was refused. Ordained a secular priest in 1673, he had no parish and spent his life seeking posts to support his extended family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He won the chair of mathematics and astrology at the university on 20 July 1672, though he held no doctorate, and taught mainly astronomy. In his Philosophical Manifest Against the Comets of 1681 he tried to separate astronomy from astrology, and when the Tyrolean Jesuit Eusebio Kino criticised his views he answered with the Libra Astronómica y Filosófica of 1690, arguing that authority had no place in these sciences, only proof. In the 1680s he made the first map of all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, and in 1692 King Charles II named him royal geographer; that year he took part in the expedition of Andrés de Pez to Pensacola Bay, which he mapped along with the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>.</p><p>During the riot of 8 June 1692, when a crowd set fire to the viceregal palace, he saved most of the archive's documents at the risk of his life, and his letter to Admiral Pez is a main Spanish account of the riot. He was chaplain of the Hospital del Amor de Dios from 1682 until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>A criollo patriot, he designed the triumphal arch for Viceroy Cerda y Aragón in 1680 with the twelve Aztec rulers as models of political virtue, and he held that the Mexican Indians descended from Naphtuhim, son of Misraim, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and that St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-the-apostle">Thomas the Apostle</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>. He learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the historian Fernando de Alva Ixtlilxochitl from the historian's son, and his attribution of the Nican mopohua to Antonio Valeriano persists today. Los infortunios de Alonso Ramírez (1690), once read as a novel, is now shown by archival research to rest on a true story he edited. He died on 22 August 1700, after asking for his body to be autopsied.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally, New Spain produced a distinctive baroque civilisation: the cathedrals of Mexico City and Puebla, the poet and scholar Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz, the polymath <b>Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora</b>, and the Royal University of Mexico, founded in 1551.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.35,-99.14&amp;year=1680" aria-label="Mexico City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1871 1447 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2321 1717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.35,-99.14&amp;year=1680">What was here in 1680? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
